<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">North American Free Trade Agreement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1994–2020</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as NAFTA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The North American Free Trade Agreement (NAFTA) was a treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> that created one of the world's largest free trade areas. Signed in 1992, it came into force on 1 January 1994, the day the Zapatista rising began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a>. It removed most tariffs between the three countries and tied Mexico's economy closely to the United States; it was replaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-mexico-canada-agreement">USMCA</a> in 2020.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-american-free-trade-agreement.jpg" alt="North American Free Trade Agreement" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicoguaro · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NAFTA_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
